")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n14 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n30 August\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n23 April – 12 September\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/358"} "Comité Belge des Jeux de la VIIème Olympiade"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/7"} "2680"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/7/countries"} "29 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n162\nin 29 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n2\nin 2 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The 1916 Olympics were scheduled to be held in Berlin but had to be cancelled because of World War I. The War was only over a year when the 1920 Olympics were awarded to war-ravaged Belgium. " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Coubertin"] " decided that, though the war would be over less than two years, the VIIth Olympiad should be celebrated as scheduled. Although the Games were decidedly austere, the Belgian people and organizing committee did an amazing job in preparing for the Games on such short notice."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Opening Ceremony was notable for the first use of the Olympic flag, the first time the " [:a {:href "/definitions/28"} "Olympic Oath"] " was taken by a competitor, and the first release of homing pigeons as a symbol of peace. Of the 29 countries competing, missing were Germany, Austria, Hungary, and Bulgaria, as they were barred as aggressor nations in World War I."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1920 Olympics mark the first time that the events were spread widely throughout the hosting nation. Most of the events were in Antwerpen, but preliminary football (soccer) matches took place in St. Gillis and Ghent. The yachting events took place on the coast in Ostend, although one event was not finished in July and was contested two months later, in September, in Amsterdam, an entirely different nation. Shooting events were held 60 kilometres (38 miles) from Antwerpen, at Beverloo, an army camp. The rowing events were held on the Grand Canal between Bruxelles and Antwerpen, while the cycling road race started at Merksem and finished in Antwerpen."] "\n\n" [:p {} "On the morning of the Opening Ceremony, a religious service was held at the Antwerpen Cathedral to remember those who had made the ultimate sacrifice in World War I. Later that day, the Olympic Stadium saw the first entrance of the Olympic Flag. It had been displayed in Paris in 1914, on the 20th anniversary of the IOC, and in 1915 in San Francisco, at the Pan-American Exposition, but World War I had prevented it reaching the Opening Ceremonies until 1920."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1920 Olympics were most notable for the début of " [:a {:href "/athletes/67728"} "Paavo Nurmi"] " of Finland, probably the greatest distance runner ever. Nurmi competed in four events, losing only in the " [:a {:href "/results/57120"} "5,000 metres"] ", where he took second to " [:a {:href "/athletes/68279"} "Joseph Guillemot"] " (FRA). His countryman " [:a {:href "/athletes/67641"} "Hannes Kolehmainen"] " returned eight years after his Stockholm victories in the " [:a {:href "/results/56634"} "5K"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/56642"} "10K"] ", and won the " [:a {:href "/results/57135"} "marathon"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The shooting programme contained 20 events, including 10 team events, allowing " [:a {:href "/athletes/44708"} "Willis Lee"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/44767"} "Lloyd Spooner"] " of the United States to win seven medals, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/44730"} "Carl Osburn"] " (USA) to win six. " [:a {:href "/athletes/22697"} "Nedo Nadi"] " (ITA) was much decorated, as he won five gold medals in fencing. His brother " [:a {:href "/athletes/22696"} "Aldo Nadi"] " also won four medals – three gold –, and they finished first and second in the " [:a {:href "/results/73998"} "individual sabre"] " event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Antwerpen Olympics helped the world recover from the Great War, and Coubertin summarized them in the Antwerpen Town Hall when he addressed the IOC in the presence of " [:a {:href "/athletes/1100006"} "King Albert of Belgium"] ":"] "\n\n" [:p {} "“This is what the seventh Olympiad has brought us: general comprehension; the certainty of being henceforward understood by all […] These festivals […] are above all festivals of human unity. In an incomparable synthesis the effort of muscles and of mind, mutual help and competition, lofty patriotism and intelligent cosmopolitanism, the personal interest in the champion and the abnegation of the team-member, are bound in a sheaf for a common task.”"] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Antwerpen (Belgium) had been promised the 1920 Olympic Games at the 17th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Paris, in 1914. But because of the ravages of World War I, which devastated Belgium, Lyon (France) made the most serious offer to step in as hosts for the Games of the VIIth Olympiad. Lyon and Amsterdam (Netherlands) formally bid for the 1920 Olympics, but withdrew prior to the 18th IOC Session in Lausanne, at which Antwerpen was confirmed as the host city on 5 April 1919. Other cities made overtures but never made an official bid: Atlanta, Georgia (United States), Budapest (Hungary), Cleveland, Ohio (United States), La Habana (Havana, Cuba), and Philadelphia, Pennsylvania (United States)."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Officially opened by"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100006"} "Albert I, Roi des Belges"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "King"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/athlete_oath"} "Taker of the Athlete's Oath"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/21227"} "Victor Boin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]] [:td {} "Fencing"] [:td {}] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies"} "Flagbearers"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/flagbearers?edition_id=7"} "Full list"]] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n" [:td {}] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Medal Disciplines"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/ARC"} "Archery"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/EJP"} "Equestrian Jumping"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/RGB"} "Rugby"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/ART"} "Art Competitions"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/EVL"} "Equestrian Vaulting"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/SAL"} "Sailing"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/GAR"} "Artistic Gymnastics"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/FEN"} "Fencing"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/SHO"} "Shooting"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/ATH"} "Athletics"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/FSK"} "Figure Skating"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/SWM"} "Swimming"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/BOX"} "Boxing"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/7/sports/FBL"} "Football"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/edit